Z||000013||Last Dodo||11-25-2001||07:57 PM||||I don't post on here very much, but I saw you're thread and thought I would add my two cents.  I had one of the very cars you speak of 88 Olds Calais International Series.  Wonderful car and engine.  If you are interested in swapping the engine into your Fiero I give it nothing but thumbs up, you will not be disappointed.  The only place the engine is really lacking is in the low end, but once it winds up it's a hoot.  <BR> If you are interested in rebuilding it you might want to check out the July 2001 issue of Kit Car.  On page 30 there is a great article on the in's and out's of nearly every quad 4 and some things that are interchangeable between the various years to include the current 2.4 liter version that is in the Cavalier and Sunfire.<BR>  I hope this helps.  And good luck on your conversion.<P>If you are interested I have a scanner at work and can post the information here, but it will be Tuesday before I can get it in.  You might try their website at kitcarmag.com and see if the article is there as well.<P>I read the article in KitCar and wanted to expound on the possibility of a crank problem in the 88 models.  The 88 crank was the first design and has a different crank sensor location than any other as it was relocated in 89 and then again in 95.  This makes the "88 crank a one year odd-ball"  Other than that I didn't see anything else about flaws in the crank other than the later versions were obviously better.  Throughout the entire article the statement that the top end of the 2.3 was the best and the bottom end of the 2.4 was the best (even with the balance shafts).  A hybrid of the two was touted as the best bet.<P>[This message has been edited by Last Dodo (edited 11-26-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Last Dodo (edited 11-26-2001).]||||reg||14
